---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { HOME } from '../../content/home';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Pump Price Canada 2026: who publishes it, how and why';
const description = 'Pump Price Canada is published by Radif Partners: official 2026 gas prices by city, an estimate for tomorrow with its measured error, no app and no account.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About Pump Price Canada</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Someone who searches for tomorrow's gas price mostly finds three kinds of pages. News sites repeat one sentence from a forecaster ("prices are expected to fall 2 cents") with no method and no track record. Price apps show what users reported, without saying how old each report is, and often ask you to install something or sign up. Official sources publish solid averages, but as tables or PDFs, weekly or monthly, with no fill-up cost and no idea of what tomorrow looks like.</p>
    <p><strong>{HOME.en.valeur}</strong></p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators from public data. It is not a public body and has no link with any government, energy regulator, oil company or station chain. It sells nothing, takes no commission, promotes no station and asks for no name or email. Pages carry no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p>The <Fragment set:html={s('ontario-retail', 'Government of Ontario weekly survey')} />, the <Fragment set:html={s('regie', 'Régie de l’énergie du Québec daily survey')} />, the maximum prices set by the <Fragment set:html={s('nseb', 'Nova Scotia')} />, <Fragment set:html={s('nbeub', 'New Brunswick')} /> and <Fragment set:html={s('pubnl', 'Newfoundland and Labrador')} /> regulators, <Fragment set:html={s('statcan', 'Statistics Canada table 18-10-0001-01')} />, the <Fragment set:html={s('ontario-rack', 'Ontario wholesale prices')} />, and the fuel tax rates published by Finance Canada and each province. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> gives every rule and the error measured for each city; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are made.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not know the price at a given station, and it does not predict what one retailer will post. Its estimate for tomorrow is a city average moved by the wholesale market and by dated taxes, shown with its past error. It is information, not a guarantee of any price.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, to report an error or a source.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
